{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length: int, last_element: int, m: int, n: int, memo: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursively counts valid sequences from the current state.\n    Uses memoization to store results of subproblems.\n    \"\"\"\n    if current_length == n:\n        return 1  # A complete sequence of length n has been formed.\n\n    if (current_length, last_element) in memo:\n        return memo[(current_length, last_element)] # Return cached result.\n\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate for the next element, which must be at least twice the last_element and up to m.\n    for next_element in range(2 * last_element, m + 1):\n        count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    memo[(current_length, last_element)] = count\n    return count\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n.\n    Each element is a positive integer, >= twice the previous, and <= m.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sequences = 0\n    memo = {}  # Initialize memoization table for dynamic programming.\n\n    # Iterate through all possible first elements for the sequence.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_sequences += _count_sequences_recursive(1, first_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    return total_sequences"}
