{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the left-most unset bit (0) in a given integer n to a set bit (1).\n    For example, for n=10 (binary 1010), it returns 14 (binary 1110).\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Determine the position of the most significant set bit.\n    msb_pos = n.bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Iterate from the MSB position downwards to find the first '0' bit.\n    for i in range(msb_pos, -1, -1):\n        if not ((n >> i) & 1):  # Check if the i-th bit is 0\n            return n | (1 << i)  # Set the i-th bit and return the new integer\n\n    # If all bits from msb_pos down to 0 are set (e.g., n = 0b111),\n    # the left-most unset bit is at the next position (msb_pos + 1).\n    return n | (1 << (msb_pos + 1))"}
