{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(arr: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair of integers with the highest product from a given array.\n    Assumes the input array contains at least two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(arr) < 2:\n        # According to critical requirements, do not raise exceptions for edge cases.\n        # This branch should ideally not be reached if caller trusts inputs.\n        # However, to avoid an IndexError for a very short array,\n        # we can return an empty tuple or similar, but the design specified tuple[int, int].\n        # The critical requirement \"Trust the caller's inputs\" implies we can assume len(arr) >= 2.\n        pass\n\n    # Sort the array to easily identify the smallest and largest elements.\n    sorted_arr = sorted(arr)\n\n    # The highest product can come from either the two largest numbers\n    # or the two smallest (most negative) numbers.\n    product_of_two_largest = sorted_arr[-1] * sorted_arr[-2]\n    product_of_two_smallest = sorted_arr[0] * sorted_arr[1]\n\n    if product_of_two_largest >= product_of_two_smallest:\n        return (sorted_arr[-2], sorted_arr[-1])\n    else:\n        return (sorted_arr[0], sorted_arr[1])"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(input_list: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int] | tuple[()]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair with the highest product from a given list of integers.\n    Returns an empty tuple if the list contains fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(input_list) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    # Initialize variables to track the two largest and two smallest numbers.\n    # Using infinities ensures that any numbers in the list will correctly initialize these.\n    max1 = -float('inf')  # Largest number found\n    max2 = -float('inf')  # Second largest number found\n    min1 = float('inf')   # Smallest number found\n    min2 = float('inf')   # Second smallest number found\n\n    for n in input_list:\n        # Update the two largest numbers\n        if n > max1:\n            max2 = max1\n            max1 = n\n        elif n > max2:\n            max2 = n\n\n        # Update the two smallest numbers\n        if n < min1:\n            min2 = min1\n            min1 = n\n        elif n < min2:\n            min2 = n\n\n    # Compare the product of the two largest with the product of the two smallest.\n    # The highest product could come from two large positive numbers or two large negative numbers.\n    if max1 * max2 >= min1 * min2:\n        return (max2, max1)\n    else:\n        return (min1, min2)"}
