{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(numbers_array: list[float | int]) -> float | int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of a strictly increasing subsequence within the array.\n    It uses a dynamic programming approach to track maximum products ending at each position.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers_array)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of a strictly increasing subsequence ending at numbers_array[i].\n    dp = list(numbers_array)\n    overall_max_product = 0.0  # Initialize to 0.0 to satisfy the >0 requirement if possible, else 0.\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A single element numbers_array[i] forms a subsequence.\n        # Update overall_max_product if this single element is a new maximum positive product.\n        if dp[i] > overall_max_product:\n            overall_max_product = dp[i]\n\n        # Consider extending strictly increasing subsequences ending at previous indices j.\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers_array[i] > numbers_array[j]:\n                candidate_product = dp[j] * numbers_array[i]\n                if candidate_product > dp[i]:\n                    dp[i] = candidate_product\n\n                # Update the overall maximum product if a new larger product is found.\n                if dp[i] > overall_max_product:\n                    overall_max_product = dp[i]\n\n    return overall_max_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def tuple_intersection(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of tuples from two lists, ignoring the order of elements within each tuple.\n    Returns a set of normalized (sorted) tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = set()\n    for t in list1:\n        normalized_set1.add(tuple(sorted(t)))\n\n    normalized_set2 = set()\n    for t in list2:\n        normalized_set2.add(tuple(sorted(t)))\n\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)"}
