{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "def armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given number is an Armstrong number based on its digits and their powers.\n    An Armstrong number equals the sum of its digits, each raised to the power of the total number of digits.\"\"\"\n    original_number = number\n\n    # Calculate the number of digits using string conversion, as described in Number.numberOfDigits().\n    num_digits_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_digits_str)\n\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n\n    # Extract digits by repeatedly taking the number modulo 10 and then integer dividing by 10,\n    # and sum their powers, as described in Number.getDigits() and isArmstrong().\n    temp_num = number\n    while temp_num > 0:\n        digit = temp_num % 10\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n        temp_num //= 10\n\n    return sum_of_powers == original_number"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(arr1: list[int], arr2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    Uses a binary search approach to eliminate elements efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    p1, p2 = 0, 0\n    len1, len2 = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n\n    while True:\n        if p1 == len1:\n            return arr2[p2 + k - 1]\n        if p2 == len2:\n            return arr1[p1 + k - 1]\n        if k == 1:\n            return min(arr1[p1], arr2[p2])\n\n        mid = k // 2\n        idx1 = min(p1 + mid, len1) - 1\n        idx2 = min(p2 + mid, len2) - 1\n\n        if arr1[idx1] <= arr2[idx2]:\n            k -= (idx1 - p1 + 1)\n            p1 = idx1 + 1\n        else:\n            k -= (idx2 - p2 + 1)\n            p2 = idx2 + 1"}
